{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the men came upon the women.</b> The commentators said that it means 'together with the women,' and it informs us of the righteousness of the women, that they consented to give their ornaments. And one must say that this is indeed praise for them, for by strict justice they were not obligated to give their ornaments — for with regard to the men, who pulled off their earrings and made the calf from them, they needed to give their earrings and ornaments to make atonement for their souls. But the women, who refused to give their ornaments for the making of the calf, if so they do not need atonement, so why should they give their ornaments? Nevertheless, on account of their love for the holy, they did not hold back from giving. But even so, the women did not want to bring the donation in their own hands to Moses, so that Moses would not suppose that they had had a share in the transgression; therefore it says <b>every willing-hearted person brought</b>, but the women did not bring — they only gave their ornaments. But from the fact that it says <b>upon the women</b> and does not say 'with the women,' it stands to reason to explain that they came upon them with great force, for the women did not want to give any gold article, lest people say that they had a share in the gold of the calf. And the men came upon them and took every gold article from them by force; therefore he mentions only the men with regard to the bringing of the gold. But the matter of spinning, in which there is no suspicion — this the women were occupied with, as it is written (Exodus 35:25) <b>And every wise-hearted woman spun with her hands</b>, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:29.944001730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}